# Every `tk` invocation the factory makes on tk itself — the subcommand AND
# the flags — one per line. `<arg>` stands for a positional argument the
# caller computes (a tick id).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The factory reaches the tracker by running `tk ...
# --json` as a subprocess instead of importing ticks' Go packages. That makes
# the tk command line a contract between two products, and an undeclared
# contract is one ticks breaks by accident on a quiet Tuesday: rename a flag,
# ship it, and the factory finds out in a container forty seconds into a run.
#
# DERIVED, not maintained by hand: it is exactly the argument lists of the
# cloudTkJSON call sites in cmd/tk/cmd, read out of the source by
# TestFactoryRequiredTkCommandsMatchTheCallSites. Adding a call site without
# regenerating this file fails that test, and every line here is then really
# executed against a real tk subprocess by
# TestFactoryRequiredTkCommandsStillRun. A list nobody checks is a comment.
#
# `--all` IS THE FRAGILE ENTRY. `tk list` defaults to the invoking user's own
# ticks, and an epic's descendants are owned by whoever filed them — a worker
# container owns what it filed. Drop `--all` and the descendant walk silently
# loses ticks and refuses legitimate waves: a correctness bug, not a crash.
# TestFactoryListAllReachesTicksOwnedByAnyone asserts the flag's EFFECT, not
# just that it is still accepted, so turning it into a no-op fails too.
#
# RELATION TO cloud/sandbox/required-tk-commands: deliberately a separate
# file, not a merge and not a subset. That one is derived from the image's
# shell scripts (entrypoint.sh, worker.sh, common.sh, preflight.sh) by
# factory.EntrypointTkCommands, so it can only ever express "the container's
# shell runs this" — it structurally cannot express "tk runs this from inside
# tk", which is exactly what `show` and `list` are, and its one-command-per-
# line format carries no flags at all. That structural gap is filed as tick
# zkq and is not resolved here. The two files have no entry in common today;
# if they ever should converge, zkq is where that decision belongs. Both are
# read the same way by their tests: comments and blank lines dropped, the rest
# is the list.
#
# NOT COVERED — JSON FIELD SHAPE. This file names commands and flags. A field
# renamed, retyped or dropped INSIDE `--json` output would sail past every
# test that reads this file: the invocation still exits 0 and still emits
# valid JSON. Nothing here is a promise about the shape of that JSON. That
# belongs to the later phase's cross-language contract artifact generated from
# schemas/. Do not read this list as covering more than the command line.
list --all --json
show <arg> --json
